# __LOOP_SUPERVISOR_PROJECT_NAME__

Describe your project here. This file, along with `docs/OBJECTIVE.md`
and `docs/decisions/`, is what the loop-supervisor agents read to
understand what to build — replace this paragraph with a real
description before starting a run.

This project is driven by [loop-supervisor](https://opencode.ai), an
OpenCode planner/architect/builder/auditor loop that implements work
one bounded task at a time over Git worktrees, with an independent
review step before anything merges.

## How the loop works

```
planner --> [architect] --> builder --> auditor --> supervisor merge
   ^                                        |
   +---------------- REPLAN ----------------+
                        |
                     REVISE --> builder (same task)
```

Four OpenCode agents, each with a narrow permission boundary:

- **`loop-planner`** (`.opencode/agents/loop-planner.md`) — read-only.
  Reads `docs/OBJECTIVE.md`, `README.md`, `docs/decisions/`, and
  `docs/plans/` to choose the next coherent unit of work, or reports
  that the project is `COMPLETE`.
- **`loop-architect`** (`.opencode/agents/loop-architect.md`) —
  read-only, invoked only when the planner or auditor escalates a
  focused design question. Proposes an ADR or asks for input.
- **`loop-builder`** (`.opencode/agents/loop-builder.md`) —
  read/write, full shell access except merge/push. Implements the
  task, runs tests, and commits to the task branch.
- **`loop-auditor`** (`.opencode/agents/loop-auditor.md`) —
  read-only plus test/lint tooling. Independently inspects the actual
  repository state and returns `ACCEPT`, `REVISE`, or `REPLAN` against
  the task's own stated acceptance criteria.

The `loop-supervisor` tool (installed as a dependency of this project;
see `pyproject.toml`) owns everything the agents are not trusted to do
themselves: starting `opencode serve`, creating and merging Git
worktrees, validating structured agent output, persisting resumable
run state, and asking a human when an agent can't proceed alone.

## Setup

```bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e ".[dev]"

cp .env.example .env
# edit .env and fill in ARGO_API_KEY and FALDA_TOKEN (or your
# provider's equivalents) -- .env is gitignored and never committed
```

## Before your first run

1. Write `docs/OBJECTIVE.md` — the project's actual goal. This is the
   first thing every agent role reads.
2. Replace this README's placeholder description above.
3. Record any known design constraints as ADRs under
   `docs/decisions/` (see `docs/decisions/README.md` for the format).
4. Commit these on a clean branch.

## Running

```bash
loop-supervisor run --project /path/to/this/checkout
```

Useful flags: `--worktree-root`, `--max-tasks`, `--max-revisions`,
`--max-replans`, `--max-architect-retries`, `--role-timeout`,
`--require-decision-approval`, `--opencode-executable`, `--max-steps`.

The checkout must be a clean Git working tree on a real branch (not
detached `HEAD`) before a run starts.

## Textual TUI

```bash
loop-supervisor tui --project /path/to/this/checkout
```

See the `loop-supervisor` package's own documentation for the full
run/resume/TUI/lock/retry model — that behavior is unchanged from the
tool this project depends on, not something this project redefines.

## Testing and quality checks

Set these up for your own project's language and tooling; the
auditor's permission block in `.opencode/agents/loop-auditor.md`
allows `pytest`, `ruff`, and `mypy` by default — adjust it if your
project uses different tools.
